==================================================
Lab 2: Basic Server-Side Template Injection (Code Context)
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A blog site lets logged-in users choose how their name appears on their comments.
The chosen value is saved and then placed into a Tornado template when a post is loaded.
Because the saved value goes into the template as code, we can break out of the expression and add our own Python.

Goal: run the system command that deletes /home/carlos/morale.txt.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When you pick a display option, the server stores a value such as user.name in blog-post-author-display.
When a post loads, the server renders the template {{user.name}}, which shows your name above your comment.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The saved value is placed inside the template without being treated as plain text.
Writing }} closes the expression early, and anything after it is read as template syntax, so we can inject our own expressions and Python statements.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Expression ({{ ... }}): Tornado evaluates the contents and prints the result.
Statement ({% ... %}): Tornado runs the contents as Python, without printing a result. We use it to import os.
System call: os.system() runs an operating system command, which is how we delete the file.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

First, we test with the expression:

user.name}}{{7*7}}

The comment name shows "Peter Wiener49}}", which confirms the expression is evaluated.

Then we send the full payload:

user.name}}{% import os %}{{os.system('rm /home/carlos/morale.txt')

The original }} closes the expression, the statement imports os, and the final expression runs the command.
The closing }} from the original template completes the last expression, so the payload does not need to add it.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Log in as wiener and post a comment on any blog post.
Intercept the request that changes blog-post-author-display, and set it to the test payload.
Reload the post and check that the name shows 49.
Send the full payload, URL-encoded, then reload the post. The lab is marked as solved once the file is deleted.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It logs in as wiener and posts a comment, so there is a post where the name appears.
* It sets blog-post-author-display to the payload, with the CSRF token from the account page.
* It reloads the post so the template is evaluated and the command runs.
* It sends the payload through data=, so requests encodes it once and the % signs are not double-encoded.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The saved display value is inserted into the template as code.
An attacker who writes }} can end the expression and add Python statements, which run on the server with its permissions.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not put user-controlled values into template source. Store a choice, such as an ID, and map it to a fixed value on the server.
If a value must appear in a template, pass it as data, not as part of the template text.
Restrict the template engine's access to modules such as os.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
Closing an expression early lets the attacker run template statements.
A simple arithmetic test confirms the injection before a harmful payload is sent.
Never let users control template source, even indirectly through saved settings.
